!! Real-world example: E-commerce data schema

This example models a full e-commerce platform schema: customers, addresses, products, categories, orders, and order items. Every relationship carries a descriptive label and uses crow's-foot cardinality notation throughout.

```
erDiagram
    accTitle: E-commerce data schema
    accDescr: Entity relationship diagram for an e-commerce platform

    %% ── Customer domain ────────────────────────
    CUSTOMER {
        int     id           PK
        string  email        UK
        string  name
        date    created_at
    }
    ADDRESS {
        int     id           PK
        int     customer_id  FK
        string  street
        string  city
        string  country
        bool    is_default
    }

    %% ── Product domain ──────────────────────────
    CATEGORY {
        int     id    PK
        string  name  UK
        int     parent_id FK
    }
    PRODUCT {
        int     id           PK
        int     category_id  FK
        string  name
        float   price
        int     stock
        bool    active
    }

    %% ── Order domain ────────────────────────────
    ORDER {
        int     id          PK
        int     customer_id FK
        int     address_id  FK
        string  status
        float   total
        date    placed_at
    }
    ORDER_ITEM {
        int     id         PK
        int     order_id   FK
        int     product_id FK
        int     quantity
        float   unit_price
    }

    %% ── Relationships ───────────────────────────
    CUSTOMER ||--o{ ADDRESS   : "has"
    CUSTOMER ||--o{ ORDER     : "places"
    ORDER    ||--o{ ORDER_ITEM : "contains"
    PRODUCT  ||--o{ ORDER_ITEM : "included in"
    CATEGORY ||--o{ PRODUCT   : "groups"
    CATEGORY o|--o{ CATEGORY  : "parent of"
    ORDER    }o--|| ADDRESS   : "ships to"
```

$$$text/vnd.tiddlywiki.mermaid
